@meta
id: 05-05
title: In code, a dot is a word
chapter: 05
type: lesson
skills: word-w
par: 25
minutes: 5
vscode: double-click, which selects only part of the expression
open: order.provider.ts

@brief
Prose is kind to `w`. Code is not.

Vim's idea of a word is a run of letters, digits and underscores — or a
run of punctuation. Which means `order.subtotal` is not one word. It is
three: `order`, `.`, `subtotal`. Press `w` along a line of TypeScript and
watch it stop on every dot, bracket and comma.

That is not a bug and you should not fight it. It is precise, and
precision is what you want when you are aiming at one piece of a chained
expression.

Prices moved under a `pricing` namespace. The subtotal now comes from
`order.pricing.subtotal`. Fix the one reference on line three.

@keys
| `w` | forward to the start of the next word |
| `a` | insert after the cursor |
| `2j` | down two lines |

@hints
You need the cursor sitting on the dot in `order.subtotal`, and then to
type after it.
--
Count the stops: from the start of the line, `w` visits `const`, `total`,
`=`, `order`, and then the dot itself. Punctuation gets its own stop.
--
`2j` then five `w` presses lands on the dot. `a` starts typing just after
it — type `pricing.` and press `Esc`.

@solution
    2j  wwwww  a pricing. <Esc>  :wq<Enter>

Five stops to cross `const total = order.` — in prose that would have been
four words' worth of ground. This is the tax `w` charges on code, and it
is why the next lesson exists.

Before you decide it is a nuisance, notice what it bought you. You landed
exactly on the dot, and the dot is a real boundary in the expression. No
other editor's word jump is that surgical: VS Code's double-click grabs
`subtotal` or the whole dotted chain depending on settings, and neither is
what you asked for.

The rule, once and for all: a word is letters, digits and underscores; or
a run of punctuation; and whitespace is never part of one. `camelCase`
and `snake_case` are each a single word. `findOne(` is two.

@start order.provider.ts
export class OrderProvider {
  public async total(order: Order) {
    const total = order.subtotal + order.shipping;

    return total;
  }
}

@expected order.provider.ts
export class OrderProvider {
  public async total(order: Order) {
    const total = order.pricing.subtotal + order.shipping;

    return total;
  }
}
